#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186091884 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "5fda8cf2-48da-5881-b3d1-834ae7d0d9c1" :knowledge-point/title "Computing One Newton-Raphson Iteration"}] :question/id #uuid "adb10509-7cc7-58fb-9c2c-5a219d0bec86" :question/math-academy-id "e-12154" :question/problem "Suppose we wish to find a root of the function $f(x)=5^{2x}-20$ in the interval $[0,2].$ Calculate one iteration of the Newton-Raphson process starting from the point $x_0 = 1.$ Round your answer to three decimal places." :question/worked-solution "The formula for the Newton-Raphson method is\n\n$$\nx_{n+1} = x_n - \\dfrac{f(x_n)}{f'(x_n)}\n$$\n\nwhere $f'(x_n)$ is the derivative of $f(x)$ evaluated at $x_n.$\n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\nf'(x) = (5^{2x}-20)' = 2\\ln (5)\\cdot 5^{2x}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, our Newton-Raphson formula is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{n+1} & ={x}_{n}-\\frac{{5}^{2{x}_{n}}-20}{2\\operatorname{ln}⁡(5)\\cdot {5}^{2{x}_{n}}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nStarting with $x_0 = 1,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} & ={x}_{0}-\\frac{{5}^{2{x}_{0}}-20}{2\\operatorname{ln}⁡(5)\\cdot {5}^{2{x}_{0}}}. \\\\  & =1-\\frac{{5}^{2}-20}{2\\operatorname{ln}⁡(5)\\cdot {5}^{2}}. \\\\  & ≈0.938,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to three decimal places."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186091885 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "4f482b25-8a5b-5aed-93bb-68c2bcdcb713" :knowledge-point/title "Computing Two Newton-Raphson Iterations"}] :question/id #uuid "35f589f7-0823-50bf-9bee-e2b9202bfda3" :question/math-academy-id "e-12155" :question/problem "Suppose we wish to find a root of the function $f(x)=e^x-x^3-3$ in the interval $[4,6].$ Calculate the second iteration of the Newton-Raphson process starting from the point $x_0 = 5.$ Round your answer to three decimal places." :question/worked-solution "The formula for the Newton-Raphson method is\n\n$$\nx_{n+1} = x_n - \\dfrac{f(x_n)}{f'(x_n)}\n$$\n\nwhere $f'(x_n)$ is the derivative of $f(x)$ evaluated at $x_n.$\n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\nf'(x) = (e^x-x^3-3)' = e^x-3x^2.\n$$\n\nTherefore, our Newton-Raphson formula is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{n+1} & ={x}_{n}-\\frac{{e}^{{x}_{n}}-{x}_{n}^{3}-3}{{e}^{{x}_{n}}-3{x}_{n}^{2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nStarting with $x_0 = 5,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} & ={x}_{0}-\\frac{{e}^{{x}_{0}}-{x}_{0}^{3}-3}{{e}^{{x}_{0}}-3{x}_{0}^{2}} \\\\  & =5-\\frac{{e}^{5}-{5}^{3}-3}{{e}^{5}-3(5)^{2}} \\\\  & =4.721\\,941…\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFor the second iteration, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{2} & ={x}_{1}-\\frac{{e}^{{x}_{1}}-{x}_{1}^{3}-3}{{e}^{{x}_{1}}-3{x}_{1}^{2}} \\\\  & =(4.721\\,941…)-\\frac{{e}^{4.721\\,941…}-(4.721\\,941…)^{3}-3}{{e}^{4.721\\,941…}-3(4.721\\,941…)^{2}} \\\\  & ≈4.632,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to three decimal places."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186091886 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "6f5b59d4-11de-519e-99fb-5490018d5225" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Function's Root Using the Newton-Raphson Method"}] :question/id #uuid "af6352ce-9fb6-5951-962a-8fe316f05c50" :question/math-academy-id "e-12156" :question/problem "Given that the function $f(x)=x^5-2$ has a root in the interval $[0,2],$ use the Newton-Raphson process starting from $x_0=1$ to estimate the root. Round your final answer to two decimal places." :question/worked-solution "The formula for the Newton-Raphson method is\n\n$$\nx_{n+1} = x_n - \\dfrac{f(x_n)}{f'(x_n)}\n$$\n\nwhere $f'(x_n)$ is the derivative of $f(x)$ evaluated at $x_n.$\n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\nf'(x) = (x^5-2)' = 5x^4.\n$$\n\nTherefore, our Newton-Raphson formula is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{n+1} & ={x}_{n}-\\frac{{x}_{n}^{5}-2}{5{x}_{n}^{4}} \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}_{n}(5{x}_{n}^{4})-({x}_{n}^{5}-2)}{5{x}_{n}^{4}} \\\\  & =\\frac{4{x}_{n}^{5}+2}{5{x}_{n}^{4}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nStarting with $x_0 = 1,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} & =\\frac{4{x}_{0}^{5}+2}{5{x}_{0}^{4}} \\\\  & =\\frac{4(1)^{5}+2}{5(1)^{4}} \\\\  & =\\frac{6}{5} \\\\  & =1.2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSimilarly, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{2} & =\\frac{4{x}_{1}^{5}+2}{5{x}_{1}^{4}} \\\\  & =\\frac{4(1.2)^{5}+2}{5(1.2)^{4}} \\\\  & =1.152… \\\\ {x}_{3} & =\\frac{4{x}_{2}^{5}+2}{5{x}_{2}^{4}} \\\\  & =\\frac{4(1.152…)^{5}+2}{5(1.152…)^{4}} \\\\  & =1.148….\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that the last two approximations $x_2 \\approx 1.15$ and $x_3 \\approx 1.15$ are the same when rounded to two decimal places.\n\nTherefore, the root of the function is $x \\approx 1.15,$ rounded to two decimal places."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092607 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "4f482b25-8a5b-5aed-93bb-68c2bcdcb713" :knowledge-point/title "Computing Two Newton-Raphson Iterations"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "171f0d0e-90eb-5b9b-aed8-770e65332933" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2.698" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186332625}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "171f0d0e-90eb-5b9b-aed8-770e65332933" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2.698" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186332625} :answer-field/id #uuid "c0949f44-7be5-5e14-b9df-8a01aeffcf8c" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186090167} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9d9b345d-b1d5-560a-9b78-65330febce09" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1.500" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186332623} {:answer/id #uuid "915cb2d1-2f8d-5ef9-bef0-487cc05f8611" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2.392" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186332624}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "915cb2d1-2f8d-5ef9-bef0-487cc05f8611" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2.392" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186332624} :answer-field/id #uuid "68461f14-52c0-5c86-94a8-579d622155a6" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186090168}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "55659377-1a68-5cde-bfb5-257e09e1e167" :question/math-academy-id "q-255772" :question/problem "Suppose we wish to find a root of the function $f(x)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡x-1$ in the interval $[1,3].$ Calculate the first two iterations of the Newton-Raphson process starting from the point ${x}_{0}=1.5.$ Round your answers to three decimal places.\n\n${x}_{1}≈$\n{{field-1}}\n$,\\,{x}_{2}≈$\n{{field-2}}" :question/requires-calculator true :question/worked-solution "The formula for the Newton-Raphson method is\n\n$$\n{x}_{n+1}={x}_{n}-\\frac{f({x}_{n})}{{f}^{′}({x}_{n})}\n$$\n\nwhere ${f}^{′}({x}_{n})$ is the derivative of $f(x)$ evaluated at ${x}_{n}.$\n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(x)=(\\operatorname{ln}⁡x-1)^{′}=\\frac{1}{x}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, our Newton-Raphson formula is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{n+1} & ={x}_{n}-\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡{x}_{n}-1}{(\\frac{1}{{x}_{n}})} \\\\  & ={x}_{n}-({x}_{n}\\operatorname{ln}⁡{x}_{n}-{x}_{n}) \\\\  & =2{x}_{n}-{x}_{n}\\operatorname{ln}⁡{x}_{n}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nStarting with ${x}_{0}=1.5,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} & =2{x}_{0}-{x}_{0}\\operatorname{ln}⁡{x}_{0} \\\\  & =2(1.5)-(1.5)\\cdot \\operatorname{ln}⁡(1.5) \\\\  & =2.391\\,802….\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFor the second iteration, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{2} & =2{x}_{1}-{x}_{1}\\operatorname{ln}⁡{x}_{1} \\\\  & =2(2.391\\,802…)-(2.391\\,802…)\\cdot \\operatorname{ln}⁡(2.391\\,802…) \\\\  & =2.697\\,840….\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, ${x}_{1}≈2.392$ and ${x}_{2}≈2.698,$ rounded to three decimal places."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092609 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "5fda8cf2-48da-5881-b3d1-834ae7d0d9c1" :knowledge-point/title "Computing One Newton-Raphson Iteration"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a80648c5-d364-5076-91b6-74f27059f5ff" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186332626} {:answer/id #uuid "925d7e4d-d537-50e5-ac6e-7917e5d90565" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1.4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186332627}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "925d7e4d-d537-50e5-ac6e-7917e5d90565" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1.4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186332627} :answer-field/id #uuid "f27c05ad-39e2-5911-a13f-0a202b1b47b9" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186090170}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "d7e60767-88af-589b-a4b8-bb73f6ce04f1" :question/math-academy-id "q-255853" :question/problem "Suppose we wish to find a root of the function $f(x)={x}^{2}+x-3$ in the interval $[0,2].$ Calculate one iteration of the Newton-Raphson process starting from the point ${x}_{0}=0.8.$\n\n${x}_{1}=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/requires-calculator true :question/worked-solution "The formula for the Newton-Raphson method is\n\n$$\n{x}_{n+1}={x}_{n}-\\frac{f({x}_{n})}{{f}^{′}({x}_{n})}\n$$\n\nwhere ${f}^{′}({x}_{n})$ is the derivative of $f(x)$ evaluated at ${x}_{n}.$\n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(x)=({x}^{2}+x-3)^{′}=2x+1.\n$$\n\nTherefore, our Newton-Raphson formula is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{n+1} & ={x}_{n}-\\frac{{x}_{n}^{2}+{x}_{n}-3}{2{x}_{n}+1} \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}_{n}(2{x}_{n}+1)-({x}_{n}^{2}+{x}_{n}-3)}{2{x}_{n}+1} \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}_{n}^{2}+3}{2{x}_{n}+1}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nStarting with ${x}_{0}=0.8,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} & =\\frac{{x}_{0}^{2}+3}{2{x}_{0}+1} \\\\  & =\\frac{(0.8)^{2}+3}{2(0.8)+1} \\\\  & =\\frac{3.64}{2.6} \\\\  & =1.4.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186332628 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "5fda8cf2-48da-5881-b3d1-834ae7d0d9c1" :knowledge-point/title "Computing One Newton-Raphson Iteration"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "186aa665-bf9e-5147-a54e-b9f6182e39c1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-0.994" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186332630}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "186aa665-bf9e-5147-a54e-b9f6182e39c1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-0.994" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186332630} :answer-field/id #uuid "eb09ae4b-1103-5001-8859-e18004577fac" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186332629}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "5fa96fb7-112b-583a-9e7b-b81a74ec73ed" :question/math-academy-id "q-256466" :question/problem "Suppose we wish to find a root of the function $f(x)={x}^{3}+3{x}^{2}-2$ in the interval $[-2,0].$ Calculate one iteration of the Newton-Raphson process starting from the point ${x}_{0}=-1.2.$ Round your answer to three decimal places.\n\n${x}_{1}≈$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "The formula for the Newton-Raphson method is\n\n$$\n{x}_{n+1}={x}_{n}-\\frac{f({x}_{n})}{{f}^{′}({x}_{n})}\n$$\n\nwhere ${f}^{′}({x}_{n})$ is the derivative of $f(x)$ evaluated at ${x}_{n}.$\n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(x)=({x}^{3}+3{x}^{2}-2)^{′}=3{x}^{2}+6x.\n$$\n\nTherefore, our Newton-Raphson formula is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{n+1} & ={x}_{n}-\\frac{{x}_{n}^{3}+3{x}_{n}^{2}-2}{3{x}_{n}^{2}+6{x}_{n}} \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}_{n}(3{x}_{n}^{2}+6{x}_{n})-({x}_{n}^{3}+3{x}_{n}^{2}-2)}{3{x}_{n}^{2}+6{x}_{n}} \\\\  & =\\frac{2{x}_{n}^{3}+3{x}_{n}^{2}+2}{3{x}_{n}^{2}+6{x}_{n}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nStarting with ${x}_{0}=-1.2,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} & =\\frac{2{x}_{0}^{3}+3{x}_{0}^{2}+2}{3{x}_{0}^{2}+6{x}_{0}} \\\\  & =\\frac{2(-1.2)^{3}+3(-1.2)^{2}+2}{3(-1.2)^{2}+6(-1.2)} \\\\  & =\\frac{2.864}{(-2.88)} \\\\  & ≈-0.994,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to three decimal places."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186332631 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "5fda8cf2-48da-5881-b3d1-834ae7d0d9c1" :knowledge-point/title "Computing One Newton-Raphson Iteration"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a26d3869-aa87-5469-8fc2-db4be27ec48c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.326" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186332633}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a26d3869-aa87-5469-8fc2-db4be27ec48c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.326" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186332633} :answer-field/id #uuid "6e4789c2-8175-5f03-91de-262539ba195a" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186332632}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "167af0d8-e301-5a6e-b854-9dc72bb28955" :question/math-academy-id "q-256484" :question/problem "Suppose we wish to find a root of the function $f(x)={e}^{2x}+3{x}^{2}-2$ in the interval $[0,1].$ Calculate one iteration of the Newton-Raphson process starting from the point ${x}_{0}=0.5.$ Round your answer to three decimal places.\n\n${x}_{1}≈$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "The formula for the Newton-Raphson method is\n\n$$\n{x}_{n+1}={x}_{n}-\\frac{f({x}_{n})}{{f}^{′}({x}_{n})}\n$$\n\nwhere ${f}^{′}({x}_{n})$ is the derivative of $f(x)$ evaluated at ${x}_{n}.$\n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(x)=({e}^{2x}+3{x}^{2})^{′}=2{e}^{2x}+6x.\n$$\n\nTherefore, our Newton-Raphson formula is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{n+1} & ={x}_{n}-\\frac{{e}^{2{x}_{n}}+3{x}_{n}^{2}-2}{2{e}^{2{x}_{n}}+6{x}_{n}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nStarting with ${x}_{0}=0.5,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} & ={x}_{0}-\\frac{{e}^{2{x}_{0}}+3{x}_{0}^{2}-2}{2{e}^{2{x}_{0}}+6{x}_{0}} \\\\  & =0.5-\\frac{{e}^{2\\cdot 0.5}+3\\cdot {0.5}^{2}-2}{2{e}^{2\\cdot 0.5}+6\\cdot 0.5} \\\\  & ≈0.326,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to three decimal places."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186332634 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "5fda8cf2-48da-5881-b3d1-834ae7d0d9c1" :knowledge-point/title "Computing One Newton-Raphson Iteration"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4a2493cd-9475-503c-b8d4-2f931679b07e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "7.066" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186332636}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "4a2493cd-9475-503c-b8d4-2f931679b07e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "7.066" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186332636} :answer-field/id #uuid "25b8cc8e-ee03-52c6-b79c-362bfe04e0df" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186332635}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "eeef1a77-6847-55fa-ad0a-a25742783b5e" :question/math-academy-id "q-256498" :question/problem "Suppose we wish to find a root of the function $f(x)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡x-\\frac{x}{10}$ in the interval $[6,8].$ Calculate one iteration of the Newton-Raphson process starting from the point ${x}_{0}=7.$ Round your answer to three decimal places.\n\n${x}_{1}≈$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "The formula for the Newton-Raphson method is\n\n$$\n{x}_{n+1}={x}_{n}-\\frac{f({x}_{n})}{{f}^{′}({x}_{n})}\n$$\n\nwhere ${f}^{′}({x}_{n})$ is the derivative of $f(x)$ evaluated at ${x}_{n}.$\n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(x)={(\\operatorname{sin}⁡x-\\frac{x}{10})}^{′}=\\operatorname{cos}⁡x-\\frac{1}{10}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our Newton-Raphson formula is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{n+1} & ={x}_{n}-\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡{x}_{n}-\\frac{{x}_{n}}{10}}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡{x}_{n}-\\frac{1}{10}} \\\\  & ={x}_{n}-\\frac{10\\operatorname{sin}⁡{x}_{n}-{x}_{n}}{10\\operatorname{cos}⁡{x}_{n}-1}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nStarting with ${x}_{0}=7,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} & ={x}_{0}-\\frac{10\\operatorname{sin}⁡{x}_{0}-{x}_{0}}{10\\operatorname{cos}⁡{x}_{0}-1} \\\\  & =7-\\frac{10\\operatorname{sin}⁡(7)-7}{10\\operatorname{cos}⁡(7)-1} \\\\  & ≈7.066,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to three decimal places."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186332637 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "4f482b25-8a5b-5aed-93bb-68c2bcdcb713" :knowledge-point/title "Computing Two Newton-Raphson Iterations"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a671634a-e152-5766-a350-c8d8e6c552f5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1.319" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186332639}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a671634a-e152-5766-a350-c8d8e6c552f5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1.319" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186332639} :answer-field/id #uuid "8758a2fa-233e-5e75-9277-b7316e2b3dec" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186332638}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "acfaee54-31cf-5487-90a0-acb4fb2b9fa9" :question/math-academy-id "q-256503" :question/problem "Suppose we wish to find a root of the function $f(x)={e}^{x}-{x}^{2}-2$ in the interval $[1,2].$ Calculate the second iteration of the Newton-Raphson process starting from the point ${x}_{0}=1.4.$ Round your answer to three decimal places.\n\n${x}_{2}≈$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "The formula for the Newton-Raphson method is\n\n$$\n{x}_{n+1}={x}_{n}-\\frac{f({x}_{n})}{{f}^{′}({x}_{n})}\n$$\n\nwhere ${f}^{′}({x}_{n})$ is the derivative of $f(x)$ evaluated at ${x}_{n}.$\n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(x)=({e}^{x}-{x}^{2}-2)^{′}={e}^{x}-2x\n$$\n\nTherefore, our Newton-Raphson formula is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{n+1} & ={x}_{n}-\\frac{{e}_{n}^{x}-{x}_{n}^{2}-2}{{e}_{n}^{x}-2{x}_{n}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nStarting with ${x}_{0}=1.4,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} & ={x}_{0}-\\frac{{e}_{0}^{x}-{x}_{0}^{2}-2}{{e}_{0}^{x}-2{x}_{0}} \\\\  & =1.4-\\frac{{e}^{1.4}-{1.4}^{2}-2}{{e}^{1.4}-2\\cdot 1.4} \\\\  & =1.4-0.075\\,844… \\\\  & =1.324\\,155….\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFor the second iteration, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{2} & ={x}_{1}-\\frac{{e}_{1}^{x}-{x}_{1}^{2}-2}{{e}_{1}^{x}-2{x}_{1}} \\\\  & =(1.324\\,155…)-\\frac{{e}^{1.324\\,155…}-(1.324\\,155…)^{2}-2}{{e}^{1.324\\,155…}-2\\cdot (1.324\\,155…)} \\\\  & ≈1.319,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to three decimal places."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186332640 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "4f482b25-8a5b-5aed-93bb-68c2bcdcb713" :knowledge-point/title "Computing Two Newton-Raphson Iterations"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b0cd3235-6427-5927-a518-24d211147824" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1.396" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186332642}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b0cd3235-6427-5927-a518-24d211147824" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1.396" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186332642} :answer-field/id #uuid "d99b0d46-9bf7-53b8-830b-9b8bbc3d3886" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186332641}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "06f30e47-4074-56c3-99fb-97fdf1cb5bac" :question/math-academy-id "q-256510" :question/problem "Suppose we wish to find a root of the function $f(x)={x}^{4}-2x-1$ in the interval $[1,2].$ Calculate the second iteration of the Newton-Raphson process starting from the point ${x}_{0}=1.5.$ Round your answer to three decimal places.\n\n${x}_{2}≈$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "The formula for the Newton-Raphson method is\n\n$$\n{x}_{n+1}={x}_{n}-\\frac{f({x}_{n})}{{f}^{′}({x}_{n})}\n$$\n\nwhere ${f}^{′}({x}_{n})$ is the derivative of $f(x)$ evaluated at ${x}_{n}.$\n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(x)=({x}^{4}-2x-1)^{′}=4{x}^{3}-2.\n$$\n\nTherefore, our Newton-Raphson formula is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{n+1} & ={x}_{n}-\\frac{{x}^{4}-2x-1}{4{x}_{n}^{3}-2} \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}_{n}(4{x}_{n}^{3}-2)-({x}_{n}^{4}-2{x}_{n}-1)}{4{x}_{n}^{3}-2} \\\\  & =\\frac{3{x}_{n}^{4}+1}{4{x}_{n}^{3}-2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nStarting with ${x}_{0}=1.5,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} & =\\frac{3{x}_{0}^{4}+1}{4{x}_{0}^{3}-2} \\\\  & =\\frac{3(1.5)^{4}+1}{4(1.5)^{3}-2} \\\\  & =1.407\\,608…\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFor the second iteration, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{2} & =\\frac{3{x}_{1}^{4}-1}{4{x}_{1}^{3}-2} \\\\  & =\\frac{3(1.407\\,608…)^{4}-1}{4(1.407\\,608…)^{3}-2} \\\\  & ≈1.396,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to three decimal places."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186332643 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "4f482b25-8a5b-5aed-93bb-68c2bcdcb713" :knowledge-point/title "Computing Two Newton-Raphson Iterations"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "44356250-45a6-53f9-8aa0-e4614fe7b7fb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2.066" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186332645}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "44356250-45a6-53f9-8aa0-e4614fe7b7fb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2.066" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186332645} :answer-field/id #uuid "8250e28d-0fbc-550b-82b7-f6e00612d89f" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186332644} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c0d075df-5acf-554e-89f3-b470da23230f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2.018" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186332647}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c0d075df-5acf-554e-89f3-b470da23230f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2.018" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186332647} :answer-field/id #uuid "d9fedcf7-700e-5ca0-9944-a5fa80045ba8" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186332646}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "9b889581-1a6e-55a5-bd9e-7cbc19420698" :question/math-academy-id "q-256554" :question/problem "Suppose we wish to find a root of the function $f(x)=\\operatorname{tan}⁡({x}^{2})-x+1$ in the interval $[1.8,2.2].$ Calculate the first two iterations of the Newton-Raphson process starting from the point ${x}_{0}=1.8.$ Round your answers to three decimal places.\n\n${x}_{1}≈$\n{{field-1}}\n$,\\,{x}_{2}≈$\n{{field-2}}" :question/worked-solution "The formula for the Newton-Raphson method is\n\n$$\n{x}_{n+1}={x}_{n}-\\frac{f({x}_{n})}{{f}^{′}({x}_{n})}\n$$\n\nwhere ${f}^{′}({x}_{n})$ is the derivative of $f(x)$ evaluated at ${x}_{n}.$\n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(x)=(\\operatorname{tan}⁡({x}^{2})-x+1)^{′}=2x{sec}^{2}⁡({x}^{2})-1.\n$$\n\nTherefore, our Newton-Raphson formula is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{n+1} & ={x}_{n}-\\frac{\\operatorname{tan}⁡({x}_{n}^{2})-{x}_{n}+1}{2{x}_{n}{sec}^{2}⁡({x}_{n}^{2})-1}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nStarting with ${x}_{0}=1.8,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} & ={x}_{0}-\\frac{\\operatorname{tan}⁡({x}_{0}^{2})-{x}_{0}+1}{2{x}_{0}{sec}^{2}⁡({x}_{0}^{2})-1} \\\\  & =1.8-\\frac{\\operatorname{tan}⁡({1.8}^{2})-1.8+1}{2(1.8){sec}^{2}⁡({1.8}^{2})-1} \\\\  & =2.066\\,129…\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFor the second iteration, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{2} & ={x}_{1}-\\frac{\\operatorname{tan}⁡({x}_{1}^{2})-{x}_{1}+1}{2{x}_{1}{sec}^{2}⁡({x}_{1}^{2})-1} \\\\  & =(2.066\\,129…)-\\frac{\\operatorname{tan}⁡((2.066\\,129…)^{2})-(2.066\\,129…)+1}{2(2.066\\,129…){sec}^{2}⁡((2.066\\,129…)^{2})-1} \\\\  & =2.017\\,679….\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, ${x}_{1}≈2.066$ and ${x}_{2}≈2.018,$ rounded to three decimal places."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186332648 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "6f5b59d4-11de-519e-99fb-5490018d5225" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Function's Root Using the Newton-Raphson Method"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8c2bc3da-491b-57d6-87df-ff4fdbdaf79c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1.13" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186332650}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "8c2bc3da-491b-57d6-87df-ff4fdbdaf79c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1.13" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186332650} :answer-field/id #uuid "bec568ec-0ddb-5c17-a5a7-3126d74f7d11" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186332649}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "e6070c72-350d-537f-91a2-dd7ce5ee69e6" :question/math-academy-id "q-256582" :question/problem "Given that the function $f(x)={x}^{3}+4x-6$ has a root in the interval $[0,2],$ use the Newton-Raphson process starting from ${x}_{0}=1.3$ to estimate the root. Round your final answer to two decimal places.\n\n$x≈$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "The formula for the Newton-Raphson method is\n\n$$\n{x}_{n+1}={x}_{n}-\\frac{f({x}_{n})}{{f}^{′}({x}_{n})}\n$$\n\nwhere ${f}^{′}({x}_{n})$ is the derivative of $f(x)$ evaluated at ${x}_{n}.$\n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(x)=({x}^{3}+4x-6)^{′}=3{x}^{2}+4.\n$$\n\nTherefore, our Newton-Raphson formula is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{n+1} & ={x}_{n}-\\frac{{x}_{n}^{3}+4{x}_{n}-6}{3{x}_{n}^{2}+4} \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}_{n}(3{x}_{n}^{2}+4)-({x}_{n}^{3}+4{x}_{n}-6)}{3{x}_{n}^{2}+4} \\\\  & =\\frac{2{x}_{n}^{3}+6}{3{x}_{n}^{2}+4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nStarting with ${x}_{0}=1.3,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} & =\\frac{2{x}_{0}^{3}+6}{3{x}_{0}^{2}+4} \\\\  & =\\frac{2(1.3)^{3}+6}{3(1.3)^{2}+4} \\\\  & =1.145…\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSimilarly, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{2} & =\\frac{2{x}_{1}^{3}+6}{3{x}_{1}^{2}+4} \\\\  & =\\frac{2(1.145…)^{3}+6}{3(1.145…)^{2}+4} \\\\  & =1.134…, \\\\ {x}_{3} & =\\frac{2{x}_{2}^{3}+6}{3{x}_{2}^{2}+4} \\\\  & =\\frac{2(1.134…)^{3}+6}{3(1.134…)^{2}+4} \\\\  & =1.134….\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that the last two approximations ${x}_{2}≈1.13$ and ${x}_{3}≈1.13$ are the same when rounded to two decimal places.\n\nTherefore, the root of the function is $x≈1.13,$ rounded to two decimal places."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186332651 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "6f5b59d4-11de-519e-99fb-5490018d5225" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Function's Root Using the Newton-Raphson Method"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0981a8d9-35d6-536e-9bf8-913c773c6fc5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1.32" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186332653}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0981a8d9-35d6-536e-9bf8-913c773c6fc5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1.32" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186332653} :answer-field/id #uuid "44d28fde-99f5-5e6f-a5cc-06f6478dfade" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186332652}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "46f3d11c-933c-5f1f-bb46-5ce326bd0899" :question/math-academy-id "q-256588" :question/problem "Given that the function $f(x)={x}^{3}-x-1$ has a root in the interval $[0,2],$ use the Newton-Raphson process starting from ${x}_{0}=1.5$ to estimate the root. Round your final answer to two decimal places.\n\n$x≈$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "The formula for the Newton-Raphson method is\n\n$$\n{x}_{n+1}={x}_{n}-\\frac{f({x}_{n})}{{f}^{′}({x}_{n})}\n$$\n\nwhere ${f}^{′}({x}_{n})$ is the derivative of $f(x)$ evaluated at ${x}_{n}.$\n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(x)=({x}^{3}-x-1)^{′}=3{x}^{2}-1.\n$$\n\nTherefore, our Newton-Raphson formula is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{n+1} & ={x}_{n}-\\frac{{x}_{n}^{3}-{x}_{n}-1}{3{x}_{n}^{2}-1} \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}_{n}(3{x}_{n}^{2}-1)-({x}_{n}^{3}-{x}_{n}-1)}{3{x}_{n}^{2}-1} \\\\  & =\\frac{2{x}_{n}^{3}+1}{3{x}_{n}^{2}-1}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nStarting with ${x}_{0}=1.5,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} & =\\frac{2{x}_{0}^{3}+1}{3{x}_{0}^{2}-1} \\\\  & =\\frac{2(1.5)^{3}+1}{3(1.5)^{2}-1} \\\\  & =1.347….\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSimilarly, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{2} & =\\frac{2{x}_{1}^{3}+1}{3{x}_{1}^{2}-1} \\\\  & =\\frac{2(1.347…)^{3}+1}{3(1.347…)^{2}-1} \\\\  & =1.325…, \\\\ {x}_{3} & =\\frac{2{x}_{2}^{3}+1}{3{x}_{2}^{2}-1} \\\\  & =\\frac{2(1.325…)^{3}+1}{3(1.325…)^{2}-1} \\\\  & =1.324…, \\\\ {x}_{4} & =\\frac{2{x}_{3}^{3}+1}{3{x}_{3}^{2}-1} \\\\  & =\\frac{2(1.324…)^{3}+1}{3(1.324…)^{2}-1} \\\\  & =1.324….\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that the last two approximations ${x}_{3}≈1.32$ and ${x}_{4}≈1.32$ are the same when rounded to two decimal places.\n\nTherefore, the root of the function is $x≈1.32,$ rounded to two decimal places."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186332654 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "6f5b59d4-11de-519e-99fb-5490018d5225" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Function's Root Using the Newton-Raphson Method"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "01bb4bb2-f929-5ab0-9dd8-bc55fb561204" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2.11" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186332656}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "01bb4bb2-f929-5ab0-9dd8-bc55fb561204" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2.11" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186332656} :answer-field/id #uuid "e0d035eb-70cb-5dd2-bddc-d57235945032" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186332655}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "f3a35506-9e8d-588e-a12b-00a24cb42738" :question/math-academy-id "q-256612" :question/problem "Given that the function $f(x)={x}^{4}-8x-3$ has a root in the interval $[1,3],$ use the Newton-Raphson process starting from ${x}_{0}=2$ to estimate the root. Round your final answer to two decimal places.\n\n$x≈$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "The formula for the Newton-Raphson method is\n\n$$\n{x}_{n+1}={x}_{n}-\\frac{f({x}_{n})}{{f}^{′}({x}_{n})}\n$$\n\nwhere ${f}^{′}({x}_{n})$ is the derivative of $f(x)$ evaluated at ${x}_{n}.$\n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(x)=({x}^{4}-8x-3)^{′}=4{x}^{3}-8.\n$$\n\nTherefore, our Newton-Raphson formula is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{n+1} & ={x}_{n}-\\frac{{x}_{n}^{4}-8{x}_{n}-3}{4{x}_{n}^{3}-8} \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}_{n}(4{x}_{n}^{3}-8)-({x}_{n}^{4}-8{x}_{n}-3)}{4{x}_{n}^{3}-8} \\\\  & =\\frac{3({x}_{n}^{4}+1)}{4({x}_{n}^{3}-2)}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nStarting with ${x}_{0}=2,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} & =\\frac{3({x}_{0}^{4}+1)}{4({x}_{0}^{3}-2)} \\\\  & =\\frac{3({2}^{4}+1)}{4({2}^{3}-2)} \\\\  & =\\frac{51}{24} \\\\  & =2.125.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSimilarly, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{2} & =\\frac{3({x}_{1}^{4}+1)}{4({x}_{1}^{3}-2)} \\\\  & =\\frac{3((2.125)^{4}+1)}{4((2.125)^{3}-2)} \\\\  & =2.112…, \\\\ {x}_{3} & =\\frac{3({x}_{2}^{4}+1)}{4({x}_{2}^{3}-2)} \\\\  & =\\frac{3((2.112…)^{4}+1)}{4((2.112…)^{3}-2)} \\\\  & =2.111….\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that the last two approximations ${x}_{2}≈2.11$ and ${x}_{3}≈2.11$ are the same when rounded to two decimal places.\n\nTherefore, the root of the function is $x≈2.11,$ rounded to two decimal places."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186332657 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "4f482b25-8a5b-5aed-93bb-68c2bcdcb713" :knowledge-point/title "Computing Two Newton-Raphson Iterations"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4baf06cd-06e4-5bab-9677-0f2a3e231967" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1.439" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186332659} {:answer/id #uuid "fcaa9737-27f8-5149-bbcb-cc33d694d67a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1.443" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186332660}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "4baf06cd-06e4-5bab-9677-0f2a3e231967" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1.439" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186332659} :answer-field/id #uuid "68bfca7d-b85d-554b-82d8-4ca24ff2ae00" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186332658}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "398d1164-07b2-5d0e-8a7a-6343a75009a5" :question/math-academy-id "q-256622" :question/problem "Suppose we wish to find a root of the function $f(x)={e}^{x}+{x}^{3}-5x$ in the interval $[1,2].$ Calculate the second iteration of the Newton-Raphson process starting from the point ${x}_{0}=1.5.$ Round your answer to three decimal places.\n\n${x}_{2}≈$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "The formula for the Newton-Raphson method is\n\n$$\n{x}_{n+1}={x}_{n}-\\frac{f({x}_{n})}{{f}^{′}({x}_{n})}\n$$\n\nwhere ${f}^{′}({x}_{n})$ is the derivative of $f(x)$ evaluated at ${x}_{n}.$\n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(x)=({e}^{x}+{x}^{3}-5x)^{′}={e}^{x}+3{x}^{2}-5.\n$$\n\nTherefore, our Newton-Raphson formula is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{n+1} & ={x}_{n}-\\frac{{e}_{n}^{x}+{x}_{n}^{3}-5{x}_{n}}{{e}_{n}^{x}+3{x}_{n}^{2}-5} \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}_{n}({e}_{n}^{x}+3{x}_{n}^{2}-5)-({e}_{n}^{x}+{x}_{n}^{3}-5{x}_{n})}{{e}_{n}^{x}+3{x}_{n}^{2}-5} \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}_{n}{e}^{{x}_{n}}+2{x}_{n}^{3}-{e}^{{x}_{n}}}{{e}^{{x}_{n}}+3{x}_{n}^{2}-5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nStarting with ${x}_{0}=1.5,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} & =\\frac{{x}_{0}{e}^{{x}_{0}}+2{x}_{0}^{3}-{e}^{{x}_{0}}}{{e}^{{x}_{0}}+3{x}_{0}^{2}-5} \\\\  & =\\frac{(1.5){e}^{1.5}+2(1.5)^{3}-{e}^{1.5}}{{e}^{1.5}+3(1.5)^{2}-5} \\\\  & =1.442\\,762….\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFor the second iteration, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{2} & =\\frac{{x}_{1}{e}^{{x}_{1}}+2{x}_{1}^{3}-{e}^{{x}_{1}}}{{e}^{{x}_{1}}+3{x}_{1}^{2}-5} \\\\  & =\\frac{(1.442\\,762…){e}^{1.442\\,762…}+2(1.442\\,762…)^{3}-{e}^{1.442\\,762…}}{{e}^{1.442\\,762…}+3(1.442\\,762…)^{2}-5} \\\\  & ≈1.439,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to three decimal places."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186332661 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "5fda8cf2-48da-5881-b3d1-834ae7d0d9c1" :knowledge-point/title "Computing One Newton-Raphson Iteration"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "13991cdc-597d-5077-ac18-00b4667fcb96" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.645" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186332663} {:answer/id #uuid "e0c503ee-cfe0-554a-bd28-75c96f11eb20" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.355" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186332664}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e0c503ee-cfe0-554a-bd28-75c96f11eb20" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.355" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186332664} :answer-field/id #uuid "8edea445-664a-52a1-9f48-4facfb64bf5b" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186332662}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "f210ebf4-a8d3-532f-b4c7-22a04d1c64b7" :question/math-academy-id "q-256623" :question/problem "Suppose we wish to find a root of the function $f(x)={e}^{-x}+{x}^{3}-2x$ in the interval $[0,1].$ Calculate one iteration of the Newton-Raphson process starting from the point ${x}_{0}=0.5.$ Round your answer to three decimal places.\n\n${x}_{1}≈$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "The formula for the Newton-Raphson method is\n\n$$\n{x}_{n+1}={x}_{n}-\\frac{f({x}_{n})}{{f}^{′}({x}_{n})}\n$$\n\nwhere ${f}^{′}({x}_{n})$ is the derivative of $f(x)$ evaluated at ${x}_{n}.$\n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(x)=({e}^{-x}+{x}^{3}-2x)^{′}=-{e}^{-x}+3{x}^{2}-2.\n$$\n\nTherefore, our Newton-Raphson formula is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{n+1} & ={x}_{n}-\\frac{{e}^{-{x}_{n}}+{x}_{n}^{3}-2{x}_{n}}{-{e}^{-{x}_{n}}+3{x}_{n}^{2}-2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nStarting with ${x}_{0}=0.5,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} & ={x}_{0}-\\frac{{e}^{-{x}_{0}}+{x}_{0}^{3}-2{x}_{0}}{-{e}^{-{x}_{0}}+3{x}_{0}^{2}-2} \\\\  & =0.5-\\frac{{e}^{-0.5}+{0.5}^{3}-2\\cdot 0.5}{-{e}^{-0.5}+3\\cdot {0.5}^{2}-2} \\\\  & ≈0.355,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to three decimal places."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186332665 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "6f5b59d4-11de-519e-99fb-5490018d5225" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Function's Root Using the Newton-Raphson Method"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4ff5bd8c-18bc-5f29-a7bd-ce648e7f6dc8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1.219" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186332667} {:answer/id #uuid "9c6e6d09-3daf-5077-9ba9-3b72ae1dad4c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1.174" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186332668}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9c6e6d09-3daf-5077-9ba9-3b72ae1dad4c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1.174" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186332668} :answer-field/id #uuid "51ebc913-9606-528e-89fa-c2c895083c67" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186332666}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "d52f717c-4da1-5117-bc70-35aea56a2d70" :question/math-academy-id "q-339531" :question/problem "Given that the function $f(x)=2{x}^{3}-{e}^{x}$  has a root in the interval $[1,2],$ use the Newton-Raphson process starting from ${x}_{0}=1$ to estimate the root. Round your final answer to three decimal places.\n\n$x≈$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "The formula for the Newton-Raphson method is\n\n$$\n{x}_{n+1}={x}_{n}-\\frac{f({x}_{n})}{{f}^{′}({x}_{n})}\n$$\n\nwhere ${f}^{′}({x}_{n})$ is the derivative of $f(x)$ evaluated at ${x}_{n}.$\n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(x)=(2{x}^{3}-{e}^{x})^{′}=6{x}^{2}-{e}^{x}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, our Newton-Raphson formula is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{n+1} & ={x}_{n}-\\frac{2{x}_{n}^{3}-{e}^{{x}_{n}}}{6{x}_{n}^{2}-{e}^{{x}_{n}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}_{n}(6{x}_{n}^{2}-{e}^{{x}_{n}})-(2{x}_{n}^{3}-{e}^{{x}_{n}})}{6{x}_{n}^{2}-{e}^{{x}_{n}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{4{x}_{n}^{3}-{x}_{n}{e}^{{x}_{n}}+{e}^{{x}_{n}}}{6{x}_{n}^{2}-{e}^{{x}_{n}}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nStarting with ${x}_{0}=1,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} & =\\frac{4{x}_{0}^{3}-{x}_{0}{e}^{{x}_{0}}+{e}^{{x}_{0}}}{6{x}_{0}^{2}-{e}^{{x}_{0}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{4(1)^{3}-(1){e}^{1}+{e}^{1}}{6(1)^{2}-{e}^{1}} \\\\  & =1.218\\,873….\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSimilarly, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{2} & =\\frac{4{x}_{1}^{3}-{x}_{1}{e}^{{x}_{1}}+{e}^{{x}_{1}}}{6{x}_{1}^{2}-{e}^{{x}_{1}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{4(1.218\\,873…)^{3}-(1.218\\,873…){e}^{1.218\\,873…}+{e}^{1.218\\,873…}}{6(1.218\\,873…)^{2}-{e}^{1.218\\,873…}} \\\\  & =1.175\\,790…, \\\\ {x}_{3} & =\\frac{4{x}_{2}^{3}-{x}_{2}{e}^{{x}_{2}}+{e}^{{x}_{2}}}{6{x}_{2}^{2}-{e}^{{x}_{2}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{4(1.175\\,790…)^{3}-(1.175\\,790…){e}^{1.175\\,790…}+{e}^{1.175\\,790…}}{6(1.175\\,790…)^{2}-{e}^{1.175\\,790…}} \\\\  & =1.173\\,749…, \\\\ {x}_{4} & =\\frac{4{x}_{3}^{3}-{x}_{2}{e}^{{x}_{3}}+{e}^{{x}_{3}}}{6{x}_{3}^{2}-{e}^{{x}_{3}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{4(1.173\\,749…)^{3}-(1.173\\,749…){e}^{1.173\\,749…}+{e}^{1.173\\,749…}}{6(1.173\\,749…)^{2}-{e}^{1.173\\,749…}} \\\\  & =1.173\\,744…,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that the last two approximations ${x}_{3}≈1.174$ and ${x}_{4}≈1.174$ are the same when rounded to three decimal places.\n\nTherefore, the root of the function is $x≈1.174,$ rounded to three decimal places."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186332669 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "6f5b59d4-11de-519e-99fb-5490018d5225" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Function's Root Using the Newton-Raphson Method"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3c83b555-37b0-5aff-b860-4d016d058146" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.14" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186332671} {:answer/id #uuid "93ecca60-c5fb-54b5-8c1d-2a4f96b41f07" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.16" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186332672}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "93ecca60-c5fb-54b5-8c1d-2a4f96b41f07" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.16" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186332672} :answer-field/id #uuid "eaa8fe1e-e01e-5701-be64-85ec7cf062fd" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186332670}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "369c5445-a294-5bc7-b712-4621aa745546" :question/math-academy-id "q-339602" :question/problem "Given that the function $f(x)=x-\\operatorname{ln}⁡x-2$ has a root in the interval $[0,1],$ use the Newton-Raphson process starting from ${x}_{0}=0.1$ to estimate the root. Round your final answer to two decimal places.\n\n$x≈$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "The formula for the Newton-Raphson method is\n\n$$\n{x}_{n+1}={x}_{n}-\\frac{f({x}_{n})}{{f}^{′}({x}_{n})}\n$$\n\nwhere ${f}^{′}({x}_{n})$ is the derivative of $f(x)$ evaluated at ${x}_{n}.$\n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(x)=(x-\\operatorname{ln}⁡x-2)^{′}=-\\frac{1}{x}+1\n$$\n\nTherefore, our Newton-Raphson formula is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{n+1} & ={x}_{n}-\\frac{{x}_{n}-\\operatorname{ln}⁡{x}_{n}-2}{(-\\frac{1}{{x}_{n}}+1)} \\\\  & ={x}_{n}-\\frac{{x}_{n}({x}_{n}-\\operatorname{ln}⁡{x}_{n}-2)}{{x}_{n}-1} \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}_{n}({x}_{n}-1)-{x}_{n}({x}_{n}-\\operatorname{ln}⁡{x}_{n}-2)}{{x}_{n}-1} \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}_{n}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡{x}_{n}+1)}{{x}_{n}-1}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nStarting with ${x}_{0}=0.1,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} & =\\frac{{x}_{0}(\\,\\operatorname{ln}⁡{x}_{0}+1)}{{x}_{0}-1} \\\\  & =\\frac{0.1\\,(\\,\\operatorname{ln}⁡(0.1)+1)}{0.1-1} \\\\  & =0.144…\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSimilarly, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{2} & =\\frac{{x}_{1}(\\,\\operatorname{ln}⁡{x}_{1}+1)}{{x}_{1}-1} \\\\  & =\\frac{0.144…(\\,\\operatorname{ln}⁡(0.144…)+1)}{0.144⋯-1} \\\\  & =0.157…, \\\\ {x}_{3} & =\\frac{{x}_{2}(\\,\\operatorname{ln}⁡{x}_{2}+1)}{{x}_{2}-1} \\\\  & =\\frac{0.157…(\\,\\operatorname{ln}⁡(0.157…)+1)}{0.157⋯-1} \\\\  & =0.158….\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that the last two approximations ${x}_{2}≈0.16$ and ${x}_{3}≈0.16$ are the same when rounded to two decimal places.\n\nTherefore, the root of the function is $x≈0.16,$ rounded to two decimal places."}]}
